WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.470
If you're a Rick and Morty fan, you probably
think you know all there is to know about

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:09.320
the show, from its obscene origins to the
fan theories about Evil Morty and truth of

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.259
Dimension C-137.

00:00:11.259 --> 00:00:15.459
But show creators Dan Harmon and Justin Roiland
have admitted that there are aspects of the

00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:20.390
show that they'd probably never reveal, leaving
dozens of Rick and Morty mysteries unanswered.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.820
So, what do we know about Adult Swim's most
popular animated series?

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.570
Are you ready to dive in and find out?

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:27.570
"Ummm..."

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.570
"Rhetorical question, Morty.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.570
The answer is 'yes.'

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.060
You just have to be a genius."

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.520
House of Cosbys

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.329
Where would the animated television world
be without Rick and Morty co-creators Dan

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:37.829
Harmon and Justin Roiland?

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:41.670
Believe it or not, fans have Bill Cosby to
thank for bringing the pair together.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:42.670
Sort of.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.640
"He was a Cosby fan at heart, it's clear.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.860
He searched and found a Cosby hair."

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:53.020
In 2005, Roiland created House of Cosbys,
an animated series focusing on a Bill Cosby

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.160
superfan who builds a cloning machine to replicate
the now-ostracized comedian.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:01.760
Roiland submitted House of Cosbys to Harmon's
Channel 101 film showcase, and the rest was

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.760
history.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.180
"I like to explore and I get into stuff.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.880
And maybe I can look around, ya know.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:09.880
Rudyyyyyy."

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.020
Roiland said those early endeavors managed
to earn a cease and desist letter from Bill

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.250
Cosby's lawyers, but Harmon knew he wanted
to work with Roiland in the future.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.750
It was the letter from Cosby's camp that prompted
Roiland to come up with The Real Animated

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.750
Adventures of Doc and Mharti as a form of
protest, which took other beloved characters

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.700
and made them do horrible things.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.130
Doc and Mharti, of course, would eventually
become Rick and Morty.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:33.219
"Oh man, my kite's in a tree, Doc.

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:34.549
I'm so sad."

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:35.570
"Don't be sad, Mharti.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.109
We can go back in time!"

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:40.250
Harmon told the Los Angeles Times that when
Adult Swim approached him about creating a

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.829
series for the network, he was positive a
collaboration with Roiland would be perfect,

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:44.829
saying,

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:46.780
"I thought about Justin because he is the
Adult Swim brand.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.979
He is the target for a lot of their stuff.

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:52.920
And he's also, like me, really passionate
about story and franchise."

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.409
"I'm gonna tell you where to stick it.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:01:57.400
Because..."

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.600
"Got it."

00:01:58.600 --> 00:01:59.880
"See, that sounds too sober!"

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:00.890
"No, it doesn't.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:01.660
It sounds great!"

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.660
"I don't believe it"

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.820
Spontaneous pilot

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.780
Immediately after Harmon and Roiland sold
their pitch to Adult Swim, the duo got started

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.930
on writing, a move that was spurred by Harmon's
involvement as showrunner on the NBC comedy

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:13.930
Community.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:17.430
With the knowledge that schedules would be
tight, Harmon and Roiland knew that time was

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.090
of the essence, and so they wrote the pilot
in just six hours.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.730
Speaking with the Los Angeles Times, Harmon
reflected on that whip-quick first writing

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:25.730
session, saying,

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.659
"[Roiland and I] were sitting on the floor,
cross-legged with laptops and I was about

00:02:29.659 --> 00:02:33.819
to get up and go home and he said, 'Wait,
if you go home, it might take us three months

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:34.830
to write this thing.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.349
Stay here right now and we can write it in
six hours.'

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:39.380
He just had a premonition about that."

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.980
Roiland described the process as "kind of
lightning in a bottle," feeling that he and

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.299
Harmon needed to get the story out, right
then and there.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:50.019
Suffice to say, countless Rick and Morty fans
are glad that they did.

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:51.019
"It's just Rick and Morty.

00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.989
Rick and Morty and their adventures, Morty.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.200
Rick and Morty forever!

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:55.659
Forever!

00:02:55.659 --> 00:02:58.030
One hundred years Rick and Morty's things!"

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.709
The accidental catchphrase

00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:05.280
What started out as an off-the-cuff remark
has turned into an ironic yet iconic catchphrase.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.040
"Wubba lubba dub dub!

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.140
Haha!

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.240
Yeah!

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.240
That's my new thing, I'm kinda like what's
his name, Arsenio.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.340
Isn't that, that's what Arsenio used to say
on his show."

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.520
While Rick first used "wubba lubba dub dub"
to punctuate a gag, the true origins of the

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.230
phrase are later revealed.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.989
"In my people's tongue, it means 'I am in
great pain, please help me.'"

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:29.280
But contradictory to the phrase's troubling
true meaning in the show, and much like its

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.329
rise to prominence, Rick's "wubba lubba dub
dub" was entirely accidental.

00:03:33.329 --> 00:03:35.709
"Know what I'm talking about Morty?

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:37.299
Wubba lubba dub dub!

00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:39.280
Hahah, Morty… that's my catchphrase.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.280
Remember?

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.690
Remember how I cemented that catch phrase?"

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.760
The line was a last-minute rewrite, originally
intended to be a reference to a bit from the

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.340
the Three Stooges, and then later revised
to refer to Arsenio Hall.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.200
When Roiland, who voices Rick, hopped in the
recording booth, he got confused by all the

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.540
changes and decided to spit the line out wrong
— on purpose.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:58.760
He explained the mix-up:

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.510
"It was scripted as parenthetical Larry or
Moe from the Three Stooges — 'wub wub wub

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:04.510
wub wub.'

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.739
And Rick was gonna fall on the ground and
do that circle thing they do.

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:11.080
And in the recording, that was a last minute
rewrite that I didn't read, and I just didn't

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.599
know what the f— I was looking at, and I
just did it wrong."

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:17.840
"I thought it'd be funnier if he didn't get
it right, ya know.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.660
'Isn't that what Arsenio… isn't that the
Arsenio thing?

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:21.750
Wubba lubba dub dub?

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:22.990
Is Arsenio?'"

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:27.320
Of course, the whole "woop woop" thing is
a tried and true cartoon tradition… just

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.750
with a Rick and Morty spin.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:29.880
"Whoop whoop whoop."

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.009
"Whoop whoop whoop."

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:32.139
"Whoop whoop whoop."

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:33.280
"Whoop whoop whoop."

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:34.340
Mental illness

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.610
The timid grandson and the super-genius grandpa
pair aren't just rooted in the Back to the

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.800
Future trilogy — they grasp onto a darker,
more complex foundation as well.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.100
On the Rick and Morty season one DVD commentary,
Harmon describes the pair as "an accurate

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.720
personification of bipolar disorder," with
Rick being the manic "side" of the brain and

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:52.840
Morty, the depressive.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:54.000
"Aw jeez, Rick."

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.620
It makes sense, given how Rick and Morty are
able to maintain a generally functional relationship

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.550
despite being vastly different.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:04.440
By the sounds of it, Rick and Morty could
very well be two halves of one whole, adding

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.020
a mind-blowing notion to the series' already
rich, complex universe.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.530
"You're a perfect impenetrable suit of human
armor, Morty.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.280
Because you're as dumb as I am smart.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.240
Which is why when I say *BELCH* shut up, it's
really good advice."

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.840
The classic theme song

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:31.660
Rick and Morty is jam-packed with sci-fi references,
from obscure '70s flicks that center around

00:05:31.669 --> 00:05:35.410
massive floating heads to movies that star
freaky horror insects.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:40.290
To imbue the out-of-this-world vibes of the
genre into every nook and cranny of the show,

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:44.289
Harmon and Roiland turned to two beloved television
series when crafting the theme song.

00:05:44.289 --> 00:05:48.790
Roiland revealed to TVOvermind that Doctor
Who and Farscape were the biggest inspirations,

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:49.790
saying,

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:54.300
"I told [Ryan Elder] I was a big fan of Farscape
and that I wanted to combine Farscape's theme

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.990
with Doctor Who's theme, and that's basically
what our theme song is.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.539
It's this amazing original piece that takes
the best aspects of those two themes and mashes

00:06:02.539 --> 00:06:03.539
them together."

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:08.099
The end result is a trippy track that Roiland
calls "super sci-fi."

00:06:08.099 --> 00:06:09.960
What's in Rick's flask?

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.560
Fans have long debated what Rick's drink of
choice is, including delectable-sounding galactic

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.100
cocktails like "super seed juice" and "Pan
Galactic Gargle Blaster" from Hitchhiker's

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.870
Guide to the Galaxy, and even 200-proof alcohol.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:25.040
But Harmon has a wildly simple theory, saying,

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.610
"I tend to assume vodka."

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:30.479
Harmon's fully aware that such an earthly
drink seems out of character for Rick, as

00:06:30.479 --> 00:06:34.970
he could easily use sci-fi tech to augment
anything he sips, but Harmon believes Rick's

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.740
addiction to his flask "anchors his identity,"
saying,

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.460
"I think the reason he doesn't do that is
because he's a little afraid he'll lose sight

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.560
of who he is."

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.349
So we sort of know which drink Rick is tethered
to…

00:06:45.349 --> 00:06:46.880
"I have a lot of vodka."

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:47.880
"I'll take one of those."

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.560
...but will we ever know why he started drinking
in the first place?

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.310
Short answer: no.

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:56.789
According to Harmon, Roiland has argued that
revealing that part of Rick's backstory would

00:06:56.789 --> 00:07:01.639
be "very shark-jump-y," likening it to finding
out the Fonz from Happy Days "didn't always

00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:02.870
wear leather jackets."

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:04.620
"Cuz I'm the Fonz!

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.620
Ayyy!"

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:06.780
The worst episode

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:12.040
Ask any Rick and Morty fan what their favorite
episode is, and they might tell you "A Rickle

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.050
in Time."

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:17.569
The episode has popped up on numerous "best
of" lists, held in high esteem for its co-existing

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:21.720
timelines, overlapping possibilities, and
nuanced commentary on morality.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.330
But there are two people who weren't so keen
on it: the guys in charge, Harmon and Roiland.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:30.919
Before "A Rickle in Time" became a fan favorite,
it was a daunting project for the co-creators,

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:35.669
as it was the introductory episode after a
15-month hiatus between the first and second

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:36.669
seasons.

00:07:36.669 --> 00:07:39.980
Roiland confessed to Rolling Stone that the
whole process was "just brutal," and that

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:42.230
it nearly defeated him and Harmon, saying.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.889
"It broke us to a certain extent.

00:07:43.889 --> 00:07:47.599
We were so close to something amazing and
we never really got there from a structural

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:48.599
standpoint."

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:52.270
Harmon agreed, admitting that he and Roiland
genuinely thought it would be the worst episode

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:53.650
of the second season, saying,

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.069
"It went off the deep end conceptually and
got really over-complicated.

00:07:57.069 --> 00:08:00.759
We're pretty convinced that the first episode
might be the worst for that reason."

00:08:00.759 --> 00:08:04.930
Considering the episode's focus on uncertainty,
it's actually kind of ironic that Harmon and

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.250
Roiland were sure it'd be a flop — and were
proven completely wrong.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.300
"Oh no, what are you doing?!"

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:11.900
"I'm not sure."

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:17.260
"I'm really uncertain about everything, even
kicking your ass!"

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:18.720
Harmon's divorce

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.360
During production of the show's third season,
Dan Harmon was going through a divorce from

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.630
his now ex-wife Erin McGathy, a podcast host,
artist, and comedian.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:31.310
The pair became engaged in December 2013,
and married in what appeared to be a hilarious,

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:36.160
unique, and love-filled wedding at Los Angeles'
Natural History Museum the following November.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:40.110
Less than a year later, however, McGathy dropped
a bombshell on Twitter, confirming she and

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.210
Harmon were splitting up.

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:45.070
While divorce in any circumstance is difficult,
there's something devastating about Harmon's

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.460
from McGathy in relation to Rick and Morty.

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:51.800
After the series' end credits, a series of
silly splash screens appear, one of them being

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:57.770
Harmon's own "vanity card" that reads "Harmonious
Claptrap" in scribbly, child-like handwriting.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:01.900
In the first two seasons of Rick and Morty,
the slate features clay versions of Harmon

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:06.090
and McGathy snuggled up on the couch, watching
television, with a cat on their lap and a

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.120
dog panting happily on the floor.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.950
Post-divorce, there's a new vanity card that
shows Harmon sprawled out on the same couch

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:15.720
alone, with a bottle of liquor in his hand
and booze strewn about the floor.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.280
It's an image harrowing enough to break your
heart.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:20.690
Ladies room

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:24.690
For the first two seasons, the Rick and Morty
writing staff was predominantly male, and

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:28.540
was described as a "very, very tiny little
writers' room with a lot of heavy lifting

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.910
from everybody," but the series' group of
scribes knew they needed a shake-up.

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.560
"You think it's efficient to get rid of women?"

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.280
"You ever see a line for the men's room?

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.400
Do you hear me, Summer?"

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.920
Season three saw the first "gender-balanced"
writers' room in the show's history, and both

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.770
Harmon and Roiland credit female writers for
playing a massive role in how the third season

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:46.770
panned out.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.700
Not only did they help ease self-doubt behind
the scenes and contribute to a renewed creative

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:55.120
energy, the female writers also allowed room
for everyone to pitch ideas for Beth and Summer

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:56.120
storylines.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:57.120
Harmon explained,

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.410
"When you have a mixed room, it's not about
the women going, 'Here's a bunch of secrets

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.410
about women.'

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.630
It's more about everyone being freer to just
pitch randomness.

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:08.310
So the result is you see cool stuff happen
with Beth and Summer this season."

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:09.310
"Thanks, girls.

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:12.400
You know, you girls are really *BELCH* something,
I'll tell you that.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:14.900
You know, when we first got here, I was like…

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:15.420
*BLECH."

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:16.420
"Get them a ship, now!"

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:17.900
Thanks for watching!

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:19.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.610
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

